00:59:36,340 --> 00:59:36,600
Right.

1260
00:59:36,700 --> 00:59:38,140
You assume a ghost car is there.

1261
00:59:38,580 --> 00:59:42,200
A census form for an unmarried person needs a real blank state.

1262
00:59:42,700 --> 00:59:47,040
If the government database doesn't have a concept of none, you might force the clerk to write

1263
00:59:47,040 --> 00:59:49,060
N.A. as the spouse's name.

1264
00:59:49,240 --> 00:59:49,820
And now?

1265
00:59:49,940 --> 00:59:54,580
And now, somewhere in a mainframe, your database literally thinks there is a living citizen married

1266
00:59:54,580 --> 00:59:56,080
to a person named N.A.

1267
00:59:56,240 --> 01:00:01,120
Which will eventually cause a cascading failure when this system tries to mail a tax form to

1268
01:00:01,120 --> 01:00:02,220
Mr. and Mrs. N.A.

1269
01:00:02,220 --> 01:00:09,060
If a language uses nulls, which most traditional languages do, it's pretending the hole in the

1270
01:00:09,060 --> 01:00:11,500
state space is just a regular valid point.

1271
01:00:12,000 --> 01:00:15,500
It conflates the absence of a thing with the thing itself.

1272
01:00:15,720 --> 01:00:15,840
Right.

1273
01:00:16,160 --> 01:00:21,300
Option, on the other hand, is a disjoint sum of the expected type and a single distinguished

1274
01:00:21,300 --> 01:00:23,380
whole, which Rust calls none.

1275
01:00:23,920 --> 01:00:29,020
It structurally forces you to explicitly handle the absence before you can touch the data.

1276
01:00:29,020 --> 01:00:33,260
So when a programmer uses a null value, they are essentially putting a plastic clothing

1277
01:00:33,260 --> 01:00:34,600
mannequin in a theater seat.

1278
01:00:34,800 --> 01:00:36,160
That's exactly what they're doing.

1279
01:00:36,260 --> 01:00:38,800
It looks like a person to the ticketing system from a distance.

1280
01:00:39,040 --> 01:00:44,120
It physically fits in the seat, but the usher's logic crashes completely when they try to hand

1281
01:00:44,120 --> 01:00:45,900
it a playbill or ask for its ticket.

1282
01:00:46,100 --> 01:00:46,340
Right.

1283
01:00:46,620 --> 01:00:50,560
By using option, we are forcing the system to be honest about the gap in reality.

1284
01:00:50,860 --> 01:00:54,840
We are forcing the usher to acknowledge that the seat is empty before they try to talk to it.

1285
01:00:54,920 --> 01:00:56,300
That is a perfect visualization.

1286
01:00:57,000 --> 01:00:57,900
Nulls are mannequins.

1287
01:00:57,900 --> 01:00:59,920
Option is an honest vacant sign.

1288
01:01:00,440 --> 01:01:02,040
You cannot talk to a vacant sign.

1289
01:01:02,840 --> 01:01:07,280
And when failure carries more information than just simple absence, we use result.

1290
01:01:07,600 --> 01:01:09,660
Result is a sum type that carries evidence.

1291
01:01:09,820 --> 01:01:10,680
Evidence of the failure.

1292
01:01:11,260 --> 01:01:14,400
Because sometimes just knowing something is gone isn't enough.

1293
01:01:14,740 --> 01:01:15,480
No, it's not.

1294
